    "narrative": "Our top play for today is **Corbin Carroll**, who leads the board with an elite 80.77 score. He faces Abbott, a lefty with a generous 5.01 xERA, and while the platoon disadvantage slightly dampens the upside, the combination of a high-leverage #2 spot in the order and a massive boost from the SP\u2019s lack of command makes him a primary target for stolen base production. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) remains a cornerstone of the lineup; he faces Waguespack and benefits from a strong platoon advantage, providing the safest floor for RBI production among our core starters.\n\nThe lineup construction reflects a heavy lean toward exploiting the current pitching slate, particularly against favorable matchups for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Henry Bolte**. **Gabriel Moreno** continues to provide high-ceiling output (66.77) thanks to a stellar 0.372 xwOBA and a prime spot in the order against a weak arm, while **Henry Bolte** (60.79) leverages his position at the top of the lineup to maximize at-bats against Javier. The most difficult decision today involved **Isaac Paredes** (45.78), who was the first cut from the active roster. While his profile is solid, the optimizer prioritized the higher efficiency metrics of **Seiya Suzuki** and **Josh Naylor**, despite their tougher matchups against Miller and Imanaga, respectively, leaving **Isaac Paredes** sidelined to manage our game caps.\n\nWe are monitoring risk factors across the board, most notably the platoon-heavy approach forced by the pitching rotations. **Austin Riley** (48.83) and **Mickey Moniak** (54.50) are particularly sensitive today; **Mickey Moniak** suffers from a significant 15% penalty due to the platoon disadvantage against Griffin, and **Austin Riley** is pushed down into the #8 hole, further limiting his run-scoring potential. While **Jose Caballero** remains active, his score of 46.26 serves as a reminder of his situational volatility; we are banking on his speed profile to maintain his roster spot despite the bottom-of-the-order drag. With **James Wood** still on the shelf, our bench depth\u2014including **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Cole Young**, **Griffin Conine**, and **Edwin Arroyo**\u2014is being managed strictly to preserve our season-long efficiency targets.",
